# Debian packaging input for the T2 channels (roadmap task 2.6.1).
#
# One debian/ source covers both T2 Debian releases - trixie (13) and
# bookworm (12): debhelper-compat (= 13) exists in bookworm (13.11) and in
# trixie, and the dependency set is identical on both (the probe decides at
# runtime what this particular machine actually supports; a below-floor
# component - e.g. at-spi2 2.46 on bookworm, which hides `desktop.*` - is a
# documented row-level degradation per specs/architecture/platform-matrix.md,
# never a packaging fork).
#
# Series suffixes (~deb12, ~deb13, ~ubuntu24.04) are NOT committed here:
# they are applied to the changelog by the channel upload tooling
# (packaging/ppa/upload.sh) when the source package is cut per channel.
#
# The binary is built from the repository tree the source package is cut
# from (see debian/rules): one static omnibusd binary, adapters are selected
# at run time, so there are no per-distro binary packages.
#
# Toolchain contract (edition 2024 = rustc >= 1.85): the builder's rustc must
# meet the floor, full stop — cargo aborts on the first workspace manifest
# otherwise. This packaging input makes NO assumption of an out-of-tree
# provisioning service: binary channel builds are cut only on builders whose
# rustc meets the floor; on series whose rustc sits below it (bookworm: 1.63
# per this very file's history, noble-era builders included), the channel
# ships source-only or prebuilt binaries produced on a provisioned host
# (docs/release-checklist.md, packaging/README.md). cargo:native /
# rustc:native stay declared as the toolchain contract; there is no
# vendored-toolchain escape hatch in this source package.
#
# Dependencies are vendored into the source package at cut time
# (packaging/ppa/upload.sh: cargo vendor + .cargo/config.toml); debian/rules
# builds --offline — Launchpad builders are network-isolated.
#
# License: no LICENSE file exists in the repository yet; the License field is
# intentionally absent from the copyright sense until upstream designates one
# (same decision as packaging/PKGBUILD).

Source: omnibus
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Omnibus maintainers <maintainers@omnibus.invalid>
# Build toolchain: cargo/rustc carry the edition-2024 floor (see above);
# gcc is the C compiler ring needs at build time (ureq -> rustls in
# kb-archwiki). No pkg-config/libalpm here: the build excludes the alpm
# adapter (debian/rules mirrors the T2 CI legs), and nothing else in the
# graph probes the system via pkg-config.
Build-Depends:
 debhelper-compat (= 13),
 cargo:native,
 rustc:native,
 gcc:native
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: omnibus
Architecture: amd64
Depends:
 ${misc:Depends},
 at-spi2-core,
 systemd
Recommends:
 network-manager,
 xdg-desktop-portal
Description: omnibusd - uniform, auditable, least-privilege MCP API over a desktop OS
 omnibusd gives AI agents a uniform, auditable, least-privilege MCP API over
 a desktop OS: services, packages, journal, accessibility UI, files,
 clipboard, screenshots, notifications, knowledge bases, network status.
 All inference lives in the MCP client; the daemon performs zero model
 calls.
 .
 The package installs one static binary plus read-only scope-manifest
 templates under /etc/omnibus/scopes.d/. A fresh install exposes exactly the
 read-only tool set; a human grants writes out-of-band by dropping
 additional manifests there - there is no runtime consent API.
 .
 apt-backed packages have no transactional undo: pkg_undo honestly refuses
 on this family (documented T2 degradation, not a defect). The daemon
 degrades along the R3 ladder when an optional component (NetworkManager,
 xdg-desktop-portal) is absent; capabilities() always reports the truth.
